  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\r\n\r\n**The problem:**\r\n\r\nWhen the HDR images become more popular a user experience problem will arise. The coexistence of HDR and SDR images will be very common. The brightness of the HDR images will cause either distraction from the SDR contents. Or they may cause the user to biased to look at them more than the rest of the page.\r\n\r\n**The proposal:**\r\n\r\nI would like to propose adding the animatable  CSS property `image-brightness` which has the following definition:\r\n```\r\nimage-brightness = \r\n  from-image\r\n  | none\r\n  | percentage\r\n```\r\nThe default value is `from-image`. `from-image` is equivalent to 100% and `none` is equivalent to 0%. This property will be applied to all the HDR images inside the selected elements. For example if a web developer wants to turn off the HDR brightness completely in the whole page, then the body element will be defined as:\r\n```\r\nbody {\r\n    image-brightness: none;\r\n}\r\n```",
